{
    "lessons": [
     {
      "id": "digital-electronics-01",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "title": "Logic Gates & Boolean Algebra",
      "minutes": 8,
      "levels": [
       "simple",
       "standard",
       "interview"
      ],
      "goals": [
       "Describe how digital circuits represent 0 and 1.",
       "Write the truth table for AND, OR, NOT, NAND, NOR, XOR and XNOR.",
       "Explain why NAND and NOR are called universal gates."
      ],
      "blocks": [
       {
        "type": "concept",
        "title": "Two levels are enough",
        "text": "A digital signal has two meaningful levels: LOW (logic 0) and HIGH (logic 1). In a 5 V logic system a voltage near 0 V is read as 0 and a voltage near 5 V is read as 1. Because the circuit only has to decide between two levels, small amounts of electrical noise do not change the stored bit. That is why computers are digital."
       },
       {
        "type": "concept",
        "title": "The three basic gates",
        "table": {
         "head": [
          "Gate",
          "Rule in words",
          "Expression",
          "Switch picture"
         ],
         "rows": [
          {
           "cells": [
            "AND",
            "Output is 1 only when all inputs are 1",
            "Y = A \u00b7 B",
            "Two switches in series"
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            "OR",
            "Output is 1 when any input is 1",
            "Y = A + B",
            "Two switches in parallel"
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            "NOT",
            "Output is the opposite of the input",
            "Y = A'",
            "A normally-closed switch"
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "concept",
        "title": "Derived gates",
        "text": "NAND = NOT(AND): Y = (A \u00b7 B)', 0 only when both inputs are 1. NOR = NOT(OR): Y = (A + B)', 1 only when both inputs are 0. XOR: 1 when the inputs differ, Y = A'B + AB'. XNOR: 1 when the inputs are the same.",
        "table": {
         "head": [
          "A",
          "B",
          "AND",
          "OR",
          "NAND",
          "NOR",
          "XOR",
          "XNOR"
         ],
         "rows": [
          {
           "cells": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            1,
            1,
            0,
            1
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            0,
            1,
            0,
            1,
            1,
            0,
            1,
            0
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            1,
            0,
            0,
            1,
            1,
            0,
            1,
            0
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            1,
            1,
            1,
            1,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            1
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "concept",
        "title": "Universal gates",
        "text": "Any logic function can be built from NAND gates alone (or NOR gates alone). Tying both inputs of a NAND together gives a NOT gate: A NAND A = A'. A NAND followed by a NOT (itself made from a NAND) gives AND; a NAND fed with inverted inputs gives OR."
       },
       {
        "type": "keypoints",
        "items": [
         "Digital = two levels, so it resists noise.",
         "A truth table for n inputs has 2^n rows.",
         "XOR differs from OR only when both inputs are 1.",
         "NAND and NOR are universal gates."
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "formula",
        "items": [
         {
          "name": "Identity / null",
          "formula": "A + 0 = A ; A \u00b7 1 = A"
         },
         {
          "name": "Complement",
          "formula": "A + A' = 1 ; A \u00b7 A' = 0"
         },
         {
          "name": "Absorption",
          "formula": "A + AB = A"
         },
         {
          "name": "De Morgan",
          "formula": "(A \u00b7 B)' = A' + B' ; (A + B)' = A' \u00b7 B'"
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "example",
        "problem": "Simplify Y = A + A'B",
        "steps": [
         "A + A'B = (A + A')(A + B)",
         "= 1 \u00b7 (A + B)"
        ],
        "answer": "Y = A + B"
       },
       {
        "type": "mistakes",
        "items": [
         "Mixing up XOR and OR.",
         "Forgetting that NAND of two 1s is 0.",
         "Writing 6 rows for a 3-input table instead of 8."
        ]
       }
      ],
      "tryIt": {
       "lab": "logic-gate-lab",
       "code": "led-blink",
       "quiz": "digital-basics"
      },
      "aiContext": "Digital signals have two levels (LOW=0, HIGH=1); noise immunity is why computers are digital. Gates: AND (series switches), OR (parallel switches), NOT. NAND=(A.B)' is 0 only when both inputs are 1; NOR=(A+B)' is 1 only when both inputs are 0; XOR is 1 when inputs differ; XNOR 1 when same. n inputs -> 2^n truth-table rows. NAND and NOR are universal: NAND with tied inputs = NOT. De Morgan: (A.B)'=A'+B', (A+B)'=A'.B'. Absorption: A+AB=A. Example: A+A'B = A+B. XOR from 4 NANDs: N1=NAND(A,B); N2=NAND(A,N1); N3=NAND(B,N1); out=NAND(N2,N3). Half adder: Sum=XOR, Carry=AND. Common mistakes: confusing XOR/OR, forgetting NAND(1,1)=0, 6 rows instead of 8."
     }
    ],
    "questions": [
     {
      "id": "de-q1",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "Output of a 2-input NAND gate when both inputs are 1?",
      "options": [
       "0",
       "1",
       "Undefined",
       "Same as input"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explanation": "NAND = NOT(AND). AND gives 1, NOT flips it to 0.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     },
     {
      "id": "de-q2",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "Which gate outputs 1 only when its two inputs are different?",
      "options": [
       "AND",
       "OR",
       "XOR",
       "XNOR"
      ],
      "answer": 2,
      "explanation": "XOR is 1 when inputs differ; XNOR is 1 when they match.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     },
     {
      "id": "de-q3",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "Which pair of gates is called universal?",
      "options": [
       "AND & OR",
       "NAND & NOR",
       "XOR & XNOR",
       "NOT & AND"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Any logic function can be built from NAND alone or NOR alone.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     },
     {
      "id": "de-q4",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "By De Morgan's theorem, (A \u00b7 B)' equals:",
      "options": [
       "A' \u00b7 B'",
       "A' + B'",
       "A + B",
       "(A + B)'"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "De Morgan: (A\u00b7B)' = A' + B'. Break the bar, change the operator.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     },
     {
      "id": "de-q5",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "Rows in the truth table of a 3-input gate?",
      "options": [
       "3",
       "6",
       "8",
       "9"
      ],
      "answer": 2,
      "explanation": "n inputs give 2^n rows, so 2^3 = 8.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     },
     {
      "id": "de-q6",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "Simplify A + A \u00b7 B",
      "options": [
       "A \u00b7 B",
       "A",
       "A + B",
       "B"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Absorption law: A + AB = A(1 + B) = A.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     },
     {
      "id": "de-q7",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "In a half adder, the Carry output is produced by which gate?",
      "options": [
       "XOR",
       "AND",
       "OR",
       "NAND"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Carry = A\u00b7B (1 only when both bits are 1). Sum is the XOR.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     },
     {
      "id": "de-q8",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "bank": "topic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "timeSec": 20,
      "prompt": "Tying both inputs of a NAND gate together makes it behave like a:",
      "options": [
       "AND",
       "OR",
       "NOT",
       "XOR"
      ],
      "answer": 2,
      "explanation": "A NAND A = A', which is a NOT gate.",
      "lessonRef": "digital-electronics-01"
     }
    ],
    "labs": [
     {
      "id": "logic-gate-lab",
      "tag": "digital-electronics",
      "title": "Logic-gate lab",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "estimatedMinutes": 15,
      "aim": "Verify the truth tables of the basic gates and build a small circuit from universal gates.",
      "components": [
       "switch A",
       "switch B",
       "LED output",
       "AND",
       "OR",
       "NOT",
       "NAND",
       "NOR",
       "XOR",
       "XNOR"
      ],
      "tasks": [
       {
        "id": "t1",
        "text": "Place each gate, test all input combinations, compare with the lesson table.",
        "check": "truthTableFilled"
       },
       {
        "id": "t2",
        "text": "Build a NOT gate using one NAND gate with both inputs tied together.",
        "check": "truthTable",
        "expected": {
         "inputs": [
          "A"
         ],
         "outputs": [
          {
           "cells": [
            0,
            1
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            1,
            0
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       },
       {
        "id": "t3",
        "text": "Build XOR from four NAND gates: N1=NAND(A,B); N2=NAND(A,N1); N3=NAND(B,N1); Output=NAND(N2,N3).",
        "check": "truthTable",
        "expected": {
         "inputs": [
          "A",
          "B"
         ],
         "outputs": [
          {
           "cells": [
            0,
            0,
            0
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            0,
            1,
            1
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            1,
            0,
            1
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            1,
            1,
            0
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       },
       {
        "id": "t4",
        "text": "Half adder: one XOR for Sum, one AND for Carry.",
        "check": "truthTable",
        "expected": {
         "inputs": [
          "A",
          "B"
         ],
         "outputs": [
          {
           "cells": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            0,
            1,
            1,
            0
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            1,
            0,
            1,
            0
           ]
          },
          {
           "cells": [
            1,
            1,
            0,
            1
           ]
          }
         ],
         "outputNames": [
          "Sum",
          "Carry"
         ]
        }
       }
      ],
      "conclusion": [
       "Which gate gives 1 only when the inputs differ?",
       "Why can a design use only NAND gates?"
      ]
     }
